window.__BUNDLE__={"generatedAt":"2026-10-22T05:19:14.960775+00:00","recentDays":7,"archiveDays":1825,"recentCutoff":"2026-10-15","archiveCutoff":"2021-10-23","addedToday":331,"addedThisRun":331,"fetchStats":{"hf":334,"arxiv":1571,"total":1905},"freshness":{"latestPaperDate":"2026-10-22","latestPublishedDate":"2026-10-22","latestBatchCount":297,"newInLast24h":331,"newInLast48h":747},"historyTotal":26665,"topicCounts":{"Multi-Agent":2926,"Evaluation":14564,"Planning":1974,"Memory":527,"Code Agent":1057,"Interpretability":906,"Agentic RAG":1462,"Agentic Search":1942,"RL for LLM":1453,"Reasoning":7249,"Hallucination":1207,"Post-Training":3027,"Alignment":3515,"World Model":526,"Tool Use":1406,"Agent Safety":2643,"Computer Use":358,"Reflection":962,"VLM Training":2070,"LLM Training":3408,"Fine-tuning":4113,"Multimodal Agent":154,"Agent Framework":919,"Error Attribution":171,"Data Engine":530},"count":36,"recentTotal":2648,"archiveCount":0,"archiveTotal":24017,"sources":{"hf":85,"arxiv":2563,"semanticScholar":0},"archiveSources":{"hf":0,"arxiv":0,"semanticScholar":0},"warnings":[],"notes":["archive is split by year under data/archive/ for fast lazy loading; data/history.json keeps the complete database"],"papers":[{"id":"arxiv:2610.29357","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29357","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29357.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":14,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29357","title":"Memory-Centric Continual Learning for AI Agents","authors":["Dongfang Li","Zhuoen Chen","Bonian Jia","Zixuan Liu","Zhengxuan Lu","Can Deng","Yukun Shi","Yuhang Meng","Xin Zhang","Jiahe Huang","Fuhao Li","Junmai Wang","Shunyao Wen","Jingjun Sun","Jiaxu Tian","Jiangling Zhang","Zihan Wang","Liangyou Li","Yitong Yao","Shuangyong Song","Beilun Wang","Pengxu Wei","Hanlin Xu","Chang Liu","Yongxiang Li","Baotian Hu","Xiangyang Ji","Min Zhang"],"topics":["Memory","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29357","tags":["Memory","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Autonomous agents powered by foundation models can reason, plan, and use tools, but their fixed weights and context limits hinder sustained learning from experience over long-term deployment. While external memory systems attempt to bridge this gap, they mostly act as passive retrieval caches, evaluated primarily by whether they can recall stored facts or past interactions. Whether memory can drive genuine continual learning remains an open question. In this paper, we introduce Memory-Centric Continual Learning (MCCL), arguing that memory should be judged by whether it improves future actions, not merely what it retrieves. MCCL has three main parts. First, a three-axis taxonomy organizes memory by where state is stored (substrates), what form it takes (assets), and how it is managed (policies). This defines a two-loop lifecycle that updates memory state in an inner loop while refining ma"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28698","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28698","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28698.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":13,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28698","title":"Scaling in Games: Continued Pre-Training For Embodied Agents In Diverse Virtual Worlds","authors":["Kuan Zhang","Yukun Chen","Zhihao Yang","Yue Su","Xiangnan Wu","Zirong Chen","Run Luo","Jinkun Hou","Tao Tan","Yinhe Zheng","Yiming Li"],"topics":["LLM Training","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28698","tags":["LLM Training","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Scaling data, diversity and model capacity has improved embodied learning in physical environments, where diverse experiences remain grounded in shared physical laws. Whether these gains extend to virtual worlds with different visual styles, dynamics, controls, and objectives remains unclear. We investigate this question through a controlled study of continued pre-training (CPT) across three axes: data volume, diversity, and model size. Our 120 training configurations span four model sizes from 0.8B to 27B, six data budgets from 50 to 1,600 hours, and five mixtures ranging from Minecraft-only data to diverse multi-world corpora. We assess performance and generalization using three-level validation losses and gameplay benchmarks from in-corpus worlds to unseen worlds. Our results show: (1) more data improves in-corpus performance, diversity improves transfer to related worlds with limited"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29680","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29680","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29680.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":11,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29680","title":"TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking","authors":["Yan Wang"],"topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29680","tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Using an LLM to generate training data for another is among the most general ways to train one, and it remains common practice. Some frontier teams have closed that route for their own models: what comes back is an answer, or a short summary, without the detailed reasoning trace behind it. That is a little ironic: they were themselves built on distilling human knowledge, and distilling an open-source model is still entirely routine; the restriction applies to their own outputs alone. Recent work recovers the hidden reasoning by replaying encrypted blocks across models. It somehow works, but it breaks the providers' terms of service, so it is not something a product can be built on long-term. We built the alternative. We take the context and the teacher's answer as input, and generate the reasoning trace ourselves, in the style of the student's own model family. Training is two-stage, sup"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.30120","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30120","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30120.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":10,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30120","title":"ALPS: Visual State Encoders for World Models","authors":["Yanqing Liu","Letian Zhang","Chengzhi Liu","Zijun Wang","Jieru Mei","Hongliang Fei","Yuyin Zhou","Cihang Xie"],"topics":["LLM Training","Planning","Evaluation","Alignment","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30120","tags":["LLM Training","Planning","Evaluation","Alignment","World Model"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"World models turn visual representations into predictions and actions. We argue that their visual foundation should combine physical-state information, native language alignment, and pixel reconstruction, so that a single representation can support prediction, instruction, and inspection. However, existing encoders typically specialize in only one or two of these capabilities; we therefore introduce ALPS, a pretrained visual state encoder that unifies all three in a single set of patch tokens. Using only still images and text, we pretrain a ViT-B/16 from scratch with language, reconstruction, and spatial correspondence objectives, and then refine it via cross-view self-distillation. In a unified evaluation across fifteen public encoders, ALPS achieves the lowest aggregate LIBERO state-probe error among ViT-B models (matching the 3.5x larger V-JEPA 2-L) while maintaining strong language a"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28887","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28887","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28887.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":9,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28887","title":"Building AI Research Teams for Autonomous Discovery of Linear Attention Architectures","authors":["Yujian Liu","Li An","Zelin He","Jacob Helwig","Jiabao Ji","Rohit Jain","Siyu Zhu","Shiyu Chang"],"topics":["Reasoning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28887","tags":["Reasoning"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Autonomous agents can increasingly propose ideas, modify code, and run experiments, creating an opportunity to accelerate scientific discovery using teams of collaborative AI researchers. To escape local minima in the discovery, we propose to encourage orthogonal exploration by constructing the team using agents with varying expertise. However, realizing this design requires more than diverse backgrounds. When experiments are costly, a team can test only a limited number of ideas, thereby creating the need to balance exploration and exploitation when selecting a subset of researchers to propose and execute new experiments. Furthermore, each result should shape what the team tries next. We therefore ask how AI researchers with different scientific backgrounds can build on one another's findings and sustain progress under a limited experimental budget. To address these challenges, we build"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.30108","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30108","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30108.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":7,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30108","title":"Multimodal Embodied Agents: A Unified Capability-centric Perspective from Computer-Use to Robot-Use","authors":["Yanzhe Chen","Ziyi Yang","Jifeng Zhu","Qiming Huang","Ruihe An","Peiyao Xu","Hesen Yang","Runda Liu","Chang Gong","Zhijun Cao","Zechen Bai","Wenzheng Zeng","Yiqi Lin","Guoqiang Liang","Kevin Yuchen Ma","Kevin Qinghong Lin","Mike Zheng Shou"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30108","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Multimodal agents (MMAs) sustain interaction through reasoning, memory, tools, and feedback, most visibly as computer-use agents, while robotic systems (RSs) couple sensing and actuation under physical dynamics. We define multimodal embodied agents (MMEAs) as goal-directed systems that couple multimodal task reasoning with physical action and revise decisions from the resulting feedback. This raises a central question: what changes when a multimodal agent moves from computer-use to robot-use? We introduce PAPAV, a unified capability-centric framework of five recurring functions: Perceive the current state, Anticipate action effects, Plan a feasible course, Act through an interface or body, and Verify the outcome. Defined by function rather than architecture, these capabilities let PAPAV identify what all three share and then compare MMEAs with MMAs and with RSs. Five physical constraints"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29458","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29458","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29458.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":5,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29458","title":"Harness Scaling for Generalizable Agentic Reinforcement Learning","authors":["Fei Bai","Huatong Song","Chuan Hao","Ran Tao","Jian Yang","Bryan Dai","Wayne Xin Zhao","Ji-Rong Wen"],"topics":["Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29458","tags":["Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Agent harnesses have become a central component of modern agentic systems, shaping how models use tools, manage context, and coordinate long-horizon interaction. Realizing the benefits of these harnesses depends on how effectively the underlying model can make use of them, motivating reinforcement learning directly through the harness. However, existing approaches typically train through a single fixed harness, which can lead to specialization and limited transfer across harness configurations. To address this, we introduce \\textbf{Harness Scaling}, a new scaling dimension for agentic RL that extends training from a single harness to diverse execution configurations. Concretely, we construct a factorized harness space, select a compact training subset with balanced factorial coverage, and organize cross-harness training to combine global coverage with controlled variation for the same ta"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29317","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29317","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29317.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":4,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29317","title":"SpatialBrain: Spatial Intelligence Growing from Experience","authors":["Yifan Zhou","Ziyang Gong","Xiaojun Ye","Yifei Liu","Donggang Li","Xiaolong Zhou","Zhihang Zhong","Xue Yang"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29317","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Spatial reasoning requires more than access to geometric tools: a reasoner must decide when direct observation is sufficient and when to reconstruct, measure, or change reference frames. We present SpatialBrain, a framework that retains experience about these decisions while keeping its vision-language backbone fixed. The key distinction is between evidence about a scene and experience about how to reason with that evidence. 3DFusionMemory fuses current-scene observations into queryable spatial relations. A Spatial Skill Library provides reusable tool procedures, and Spatial Cognition associates task conditions with experience about when and how to use memory and skills. Completed trajectories are abstracted into process records and consolidated between task blocks, allowing later decisions to draw on earlier execution without transferring scene coordinates or reference answers. Across f"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28763","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28763","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28763.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":4,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28763","title":"Automating ML for Science: Can Frontier Agents Climb Scientific Hills in the Wild?","authors":["Ming Zhong","Stacy Li","Nicholas Carlini","Matthew Jagielski"],"topics":["Code Agent","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28763","tags":["Code Agent","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Frontier coding agents are increasingly framed as end-to-end automated researchers, and ML for science offers a demanding test of that promise: improving a scientific model often requires domain insight beyond routine ML engineering. We introduce Agent4Sci, a long-horizon testbed for measuring how far today's agents can improve the published best models on scientific ML benchmarks, spanning 20 benchmarks across 11 domains. Rather than ranking agents on a leaderboard, Agent4Sci asks what kinds of progress agents can make on these tasks under in-the-wild conditions, and where they fall short. Empirically, today's frontier agents advance the published best on every benchmark, with Claude Opus reaching 10.4% mean relative improvement and no run flagged for academic misconduct. These gains, however, come almost entirely from a bag of ML-engineering tricks rather than research-level insight. T"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28711","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.28711","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28711.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":3,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28711","title":"Qwen-Audio-3.1-ASR Technical Report","authors":["Chuanmeng Bian","Daren Chen","Peixin Chen","Zhigao Chen","Chong Deng","Zhiyun Fan","Zhifu Gao","Bo Gong","Qing Gu","Jiajun He","Yawei Hu","Yunjie Ji","Wei Ju","Jingbei Li","Xiangang Li","Xu Li","Zengxi Li","Zheng Li","Chengdong Liang","Baiji Liu","Yaming Liu","Ying Liu","Bin Ma","Yiping Peng","Yuezhang Peng","Zhendong Peng","Yu Pu","Yang Shi","Xin Shu","Jian Tang","Biao Tian","Peiyao Wang","Tianzi Wang","Wen Wang","Wupeng Wang","Cheng Wen","Yuzhong Wu","Zijian Xia","Yunchong Xiao","Nan Yang","Jianwei Yu","Jixing Yu","Binbin Zhang","Lei Zhang","Sitong Zhao","Guangdong Zhou","Yuan Zhou","Jianheng Zhuo"],"topics":["Reasoning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28711","tags":["Reasoning"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"We present Qwen-Audio-3.1-ASR, a family of audio-language models that builds upon its predecesor Qwen-Audio-3.0-ASR with improved speech recognition and expanded capabilities in speaker-diarized transcription and general audio understanding. The family retains support for 30 languages and 16 Chinese dialects, together with instruction-controlled capabilities including historical-context conditioning, hierarchical hotword customization, and native transcription polishing. Speaker-diarized ASR produces structured outputs containing speaker labels, timestamps, and recognized text, maintaining consistent speaker labels across turns and the representation of overlapping speech. The general audio understanding capabilities cover audio-, speech-, and music-captioning, temporal grounding and sound event detection, as well as audio question answering and multiple-choice reasoning. These tasks bro"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.30136","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30136","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30136.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":2,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30136","title":"Agentic World Action Models","authors":["Zhuoyang Liu","Dantong Niu","Botong Zhang","Boning Shao","Jing Wang","Ruijie Zheng","Yuvan Sharma","Runyu Lu","Jiaming Liu","Renrui Zhang","Roei Herzig","Yutong Bai","Ken Goldberg","Danfei Xu","Yuke Zhu","Guanzhi Wang","Linxi \"Jim\" Fan","Trevor Darrell"],"topics":["Agent Safety","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30136","tags":["Agent Safety","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"source":"hf","abstract":"Recent world action models provide visual foresight and future prediction for robot manipulation, but lack explicit mechanisms for compositional skill reuse. Agentic robotics combines code-based planning and reasoning with reusable skills, but often lacks predictive grounding in physical dynamics. We introduce AW-0, an agentic world action model framework that integrates code-based planning with joint video and action prediction. We jointly model code, future video, and robot actions as three coupled generative streams within a unified mixture-of-transformers architecture. Within this architecture a code expert generates parameterized skill routines and control logic while video and action experts generate future visual states and low-level motor commands. Code serves two complementary roles: it structures multi-step prediction and control within the world model and represents skills as "},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29784","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29784","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29784.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":2,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29784","title":"Evolving with Open Worlds: Towards Recursive Self-Improvement for Future Forecasting","authors":["Zhipeng Chen","Yunsen Liang","Yanxi Chen","Hongkang Zhao","Lingyun Feng","Ruiyang Ren","Guoxin Chen","Jia Deng","Feng Chang","Chuan Hao","Ran Tao","Jian Yang","Ji-Rong Wen","Bryan Dai","Wayne Xin Zhao"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29784","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Most contemporary artificial intelligence (AI) systems are released with frozen parameters and improve through external updates, whereas an ideal system would keep improving from its own experience, a capacity we call recursive self-improvement (RSI). Yet studying RSI requires an environment that is continuously changing, objectively verifiable, and naturally grounded, posing a fundamental challenge for agent evolution. Here we introduce DeepEvolve, a sustainable evolution agent system grounded in future event prediction, in which agents continuously interact with a dynamically evolving environment and improve by internalizing and externalizing the experience gained through these interactions. A living environment synthesizes verifiable tasks, and a recursive-evolution engine turns the reasoning trajectory behind each prediction back into capability: decoupling search from prediction ada"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29968","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.29968","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29968.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":2,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29968","title":"CulturalRiddles: A Multicultural Benchmark of Riddles","authors":["My Chiffon Nguyen","Tianyi Hu","Vinod Anbalagan","Henry Gagnier","Pouya Sadeghi","Farah Abdou","Tidor-Vlad Pricope","Diego MMS Oliveira","Jocelyn K D'Arcy","Madeline Smith","Oluwaseun A Ajayi","Yusser Al Ghussin","Zena Al-Khalili","Micol Altomare","Shayan Amir","Ahanaf Aziz","Bryan Chen","Ruchira Dhar","Abdellah El Mekki","Rawan Mohammed Elframawy","Akhil Eppa","Silvia Fernandez","Alex Gimei","Arya Hariharan","Christian Hoang","RamMohanRao Kadiyala","Diana Kozachek","Peerat Limkonchotiwat","Ankita Maity","Di Mo","Ayush Munot","Aylin Naebzadeh","Anastasiia Nikitina","Dina Pisarevskaya","Divas Poudel","Jebish Purbey","Md Rabius Sany Apu","Ahmad Mufli Ramadhan","Ralitza Nikolova Rizova","Diogo M. Serrano-Miranda","Vivek Gopalkrishna Silimkhan","Luis Fernando Solis Navarro","Kaiser Sun","Arya Suneesh","Manaswitha Sunkara","Tejaswi Sunkara","Jonathan E Swindell","Hanna Yukhymenko","Jingyi Zheng","Esther Adenuga","Ilia Afanasev","Wafa Aissa","Lisa Alazraki","Lauren C Altomare","Priscilla Amondi Amuok","Emile Anand","Gabriel Assis","José Matías Avalos","Silvia Lorena Avila","Zakaria Baannou","Niyati Bafna","Yash Bagla","Sarah Bahrami","Bontu F Balcha","Vanya Bannihatti Kumar","Irene Baucells","Abdumutalib Begmuratov","Naman Bhatia","Souvik Bhattacharyya","Isabel Blanco Ferreiro","Felipe Bochini","Sayuru Bopitiya","Angana Borah","Tulika Bose","Olena Bugaiova","Juliana Carvalho","Nancy F Chen","Stephy Chen","Rochelle Choenni","Jayanth Krishna Chundru","Leandro A Cossio","Nebojša Damnjanović","Ona de Gibert","Marco De Santis","Pedro Delgado","Kaustubh Dholé","Adnan El Assadi","Wesam El-Sayed","Nadine M Elframawy","Oumayma Essarhi","Naome A Etori","Reuben Chagas Fernandes","Saanidhi Pravin Gade","Javier García Gilabert","Álvaro González-Escapa","Bin Hu","Kaiyu Huang","Abderrahmane Issam","Danka Jokić","Ulugbek Khalilov","Dominik Krzemiński","Roy Ka-Wei Lee","Haichang Li","Chen Cecilia Liu","Zhengyuan Liu","Cedric Lothritz","Houman Mehrafarin","Antonio Miranda-Escalada","Fengran Mo","Mehrnaz Mofakhami","Wafaa Mohammed","Felipe Moreno-Vera","Prahitha Movva","Jannatul Nayem","Van Ngo","Joy Olusanya","Marc Pàmies","Avinash Kuney Pandey","Yurii Paniv","Fred Philippy","Vladislav Poritski","Dikshya Poudel","Joanna Radoła","Sara Rajaee","Swati Rajwal","Prerana Rane","Muhammad Ravi Shulthan Habibi","Lucas Resck","Shubhashis Roy Dipta","Ananya Sahu","Eduardo Sánchez","Borja Sánchez Clemente","Aleix Sant","Nobin Sarwar","Istiak Shihab","Santhoshini Pranathi Singaraju","Jane Smith","David Stap","Emily Z Su","Ananth K Suresh","Sajag Swami","Ruiyang Tan","Nazia Tasnim","Khalid Tounoussi","Patteera Triamamornwooth","Muhammad Saad Umar","Shankar Venkitachalam","Oksana Volchek","Lingbo Wang","Debela Desalegn Yadeta","Rushikesh Zawar","Jinghan Zhang","Mike Zhang","Weihua Zheng","Marzieh Fadaee","Akhil Arora","Károly Boczka","Julia Kreutzer"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29968","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"As LLMs expand their language coverage, evaluating how well they understand and represent diverse cultures becomes increasingly important. However, many benchmarks remain English-centric, with limited cultural coverage and a focus on factual knowledge over its application in real-world cultural contexts. We propose CulturalRiddles, a benchmark of original, culturally grounded riddles created entirely by humans through a participatory community effort, spanning 62 cultures (45 countries, 51 languages). These riddles are deeply rooted in their respective cultures and require abductive and compositional reasoning, alongside detailed factual and social knowledge, language skills, and an understanding of culturally specific associations. We benchmark 28 LLM configurations using both automatic and large-scale human evaluation. Even frontier models achieve at most 22% accuracy in some communiti"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.30090","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.30090","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30090.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":2,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30090","title":"VX-One: Typed, Calibrated Decision Heads for Real-Time Perimeter Triage - An empirical study of unknown-attack honesty, cascade gray zones, and calibration under drift","authors":["Juan S. Santillana"],"topics":["Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30090","tags":["Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"hf","abstract":"Perimeter triage faces a latency/semantics trade-off: signature engines are fast and format-safe but blind to unseen attacks, while generative models are semantic but slow and produce unparseable output. In late 2026 a class of commercial System One decision models (TypeSafe Jev; Microsoft-Decision-1) began selling typed decision primitives - binary gates, closed-set choices, ordinal scores - as product APIs, with no academic treatment. We present VX-One: a small multi-head network (100K-10M parameters) whose outputs are these primitives - a calibrated gate, a choice over a closed candidate set that always includes an explicit unknown option, and an ordinal score, trained multi-task, temperature-calibrated per head (~0.7-1.7 ms p50 on CPU). We evaluate it on UNSW-NB15, CIC-IDS2017, EMBER2024 against calibrated XGBoost, Random-Forest, MLP, Kitsune, and measure a two-phase cascade (Suricat"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.26542","submittedDate":"2026-10-19","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.26542","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.26542.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":19,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26542","title":"Breaking Diffusion: Parallel Reasoning with Draft Refinement","authors":["Jaesang Yu","Seoyeon Byun","Yun Wang","Yan Lu","Juho Kim"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26542","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Drafting an answer allows a reasoning model to reconsider each decision in the context of the others. Yet repeated diffusion denoising does not ensure that individually plausible token updates form a better answer together. We introduce Draft Refinement Language Models (DRLMs), which use a single denoiser to propose parallel revisions and evaluate a candidate draft. We train the denoiser to reconstruct every token from uniformly corrupted drafts, teaching both preservation and repair. Its predictions specify revisions directly, without a diffusion reverse transition. Evaluation requires a different use of these predictions: confidence in an existing token reflects both contextual support and evidence from observing the token itself. Using the known corruption process, we correct for this self-token evidence to estimate how well each token is supported by the prompt and the remaining draf"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29139","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29139.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29139","title":"Steering Black-Box Large Language Model Agents with Pitfall Anticipation World Model","authors":["Jiongxiao Wang","Han Ding","Youzhi Luo","Yawei Wang","Yun Zhou","Linbo Liu","Panpan Xu"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning","Data Engine","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29139","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning","Data Engine"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Frontier black-box Large Language Model (LLM) agents are proficient at long-horizon reasoning and precise action generation. However, when it comes to a specific agentic task, they have no internalized knowledge of how that particular environment unfolds. A world model offers a natural remedy, providing the agent with environment specific knowledge by foreseeing how the environment will respond to the action it is about to take. Prior world models for LLM agents mainly predict next observations at each step, which is costly and often uninformative because individual observations provide limited task-relevant guidance and not every step requires call for foresight. This raises a key question: what a world model should predict and when should it be invoked for agentic tasks? Our pilot study suggests that world model guidance is most useful when it anticipates pitfalls within a bounded mult"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29010","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29010.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29010","title":"An Evidence-Driven Multi-Agent Compound AI System for Head and Neck Proton Radiotherapy Planning","authors":["Qingqing Wang","Liqiang Xiao","Chang Chang"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29010","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Planning"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Radiation treatment planning requires balancing competing objectives to satisfy clinical criteria while accommodating physicians' preferences. This study aims to develop an evidence-driven, multi-agent compound AI system that integrates DRL-based automated treatment planning with LLM-based conversational plan refinement for proton PBS therapy. A policy network trained using PPO first generates multiple plans without human intervention, from which one is selected as the target plan and the others serve as references for subsequent refinements. For challenging cases requiring further adjustments, an LLM-based multi-agent system enables plan refinement through natural language interactions. The system comprises a planning agent that recommends evidence-supported adjustments, a chatbot providing a unified conversational interface, and an evaluation agent that retrieves clinical metrics and i"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29189","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29189.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29189","title":"Reasoning-Search-Augmented LLMs: An Operational Taxonomy, Benchmark Analysis and Controlled Evidence Synthesis","authors":["Biswas Poudel","Nilson Chapagain","Amit Kumar","Xianshun Jiang"],"topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Planning","Hallucination","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29189","tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Planning","Hallucination","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive emergent reasoning capabilities, yet they remain constrained by fixed model parameters and finite context windows. Even when augmented with static retrieval, they can produce statements that are unsupported by, or even contradictory to, retrieved evidence. This leads to hallucinations, difficulty incorporating up-to-date information, and failures to correctly interpret or reconcile sources. Recent work addresses these limitations by tightly coupling step-by-step reasoning with interactive search, treating retrieval as an action within an iterative agentic loop rather than a one-shot preprocessing step. This study reviews frameworks that implement such reasoning-search loops by interleaving planning, querying, retrieval, integration, verification, and stopping. We analyze these systems through four architectural axes: retrieval coupling,"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.30099","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30099.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30099","title":"Differentially Private Sparse Reward Estimation with Preference Feedback","authors":["Meng Ding","Mingxi Lei","Jie Zhang","Jinyan Liu","Di Wang"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30099","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment","RL for LLM"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Reinforcement Learning with Human Feedback (RLHF) has become a central paradigm for aligning Large Language Models with human values. However, the reliance on sensitive preference data necessitates rigorous differential privacy (DP) guarantees to prevent user information leakage. Existing private alignment methods typically assume dense reward parameterizations, resulting in estimation error bounds that scale polynomially with the ambient {model size $d$}. Such dependence is prohibitive in modern {overparameterized models} where $d$ is vast. In this work, we study \\emph{Private Sparse RLHF} problem to address this limitation, assuming that human preferences (reward model) are governed by a subset of $s^* \\ll d$ relevant features. We provide the first comprehensive theoretical framework for differentially private sparse reward estimation from pairwise preference feedback under the Bradley"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28955","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28955.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28955","title":"SLoSa: Small, Low-Power, and Safe Foundation Models for Robotic Task Planning","authors":["Weizhe Xu","Jialiang Fan","Essien Taylor","Zijun Wang","Russell Joseph","Fanxin Kong"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28955","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Safe robotic task planning requires planners that can reliably achieve task goals while satisfying safety constraints. Classical planners search over explicit symbolic task representations and can provide formal guarantees with respect to encoded safety constraints, but require rigid input formats. Their computational cost also increases rapidly with the search space. In contrast, large language models (LLMs) offer flexible task interfaces and strong generalization capabilities, but they are large, power-hungry, and pre-trained with little robot-specific safety data and knowledge. Thus, they can be difficult to deploy locally on robotic systems with limited resources. To address these limitations, we present SLoSa, a neuro-symbolic compression and fine-tuning framework for training foundation-model-based robotic task planners with Small size, Low power demand, and Safety awareness. The f"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.30132","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30132.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30132","title":"LongZero: Enhancing Long-Context Reasoning in LLMs from Zero Data","authors":["Zhennan Wan","Jianfei Chen"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30132","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Reinforcement learning (RL) is an effective way to strengthen the long-context reasoning of large language models (LLMs), but it needs training data that are long, diverse, challenging, and verifiable. Human annotation of such data is expensive, naturally occurring data are too scarce to sustain RL, and existing synthesis methods depend on external corpora or stronger teacher models, which become harder to obtain as the trained models get stronger. Recently, RL from zero data, in which a model learns from tasks it proposes itself, has achieved excellent performance, but existing methods target short-context tasks. Therefore, we propose $\\textbf{LongZero}$, a method that lets an LLM improve its own long-context reasoning without any external data. Instead of writing questions and answers over existing documents, LongZero first has the model synthesize the question and answer with its supp"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29995","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29995.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29995","title":"Remembering Through Change: Provenance-Preserving Hypergraph Memory for Multimodal Agents","authors":["Jielong Tang","Ling-An Zeng","Xujie Yuan","Xiao Dong","Zhiqian Xia","Qingfeng Li","Shixiong Kai","Kaichao Liang","Rong Yao","Jian Yin"],"topics":["Agentic Search","Reasoning","Memory","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29995","tags":["Agentic Search","Reasoning","Memory","Evaluation","Multimodal Agent"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Long-term memory for multimodal agents remains challenging, as it must preserve fine-grained visual evidence while tracking entity evolution across sessions. Existing abstraction-based memories compress images into text, often discarding visually discriminative details, whereas pixel-preserving memories retain raw observations but struggle to reliably associate diverse observations with persistent entity identities and track their evolving states. To address these limitations, we propose MHG-Mem, a multimodal hypergraph memory that jointly organizes entity identity, state evolution, and provenance to visual evidence. Specifically, MHG-Mem represents distinct entity mentions and their observed facts as individual nodes, grouping identity-consistent mentions and facts through occurrence hyperedges. It then synthesizes aspect-specific evolution states from these grouped observations and lin"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29346","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29346.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29346","title":"Benchmarking and Error Analysis of Deep Search and Deep Research LLM Agents","authors":["Zixuan Yu","Zhenheng Tang","Xiaowen Chu","Bo Han"],"topics":["Agentic Search","Agent Safety","Error Attribution","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29346","tags":["Agentic Search","Agent Safety","Error Attribution","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) evolve into autonomous Deep Search and Deep Research Agents capable of executing long-horizon, multi-step tasks, evaluating their reliability becomes increasingly critical. However, existing evaluation paradigms predominantly focus on final task outcomes, leaving a significant gap in understanding the intermediate execution traces, where agents are highly susceptible to various errors. To address this critical gap, we introduce a comprehensive methodology and analytical framework designed specifically to diagnose errors in intermediate workflows. Building upon this framework, we establish a structured taxonomy that categorizes these vulnerabilities into two primary dimensions: System-Level Errors and Task-Level Errors. Through a systematic examination of step-by-step execution traces and agent-environment interactions, we reveal how these underlying errors"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29590","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29590.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29590","title":"CineEvolve: An Agentic Self-Evolution Framework for Cinematic Multi-Shot Video Generation","authors":["Jingzhe Zhang","Peixuan Zhang","Genying Li","Bing Ma","Shuchen Weng","Si Li","Boxin Shi"],"topics":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29590","tags":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Reflection"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Cinematic multi-shot video generation requires both narrative planning and iterative refinement. Existing methods either lack narrative reasoning or rely on feed-forward workflows without policy updates. In this paper, we propose CineEvolve, an agentic self-evolution framework for cinematic multi-shot video generation that unifies a Director and a Critic within a single vision-language policy. Emulating real-world filmmaking that couples iterative direction with critical feedback, we train CineEvolve via alternating reinforcement learning, where the Director surfaces flaws to sharpen the Critic, and the updated Critic guides the Director's targeted revisions. To stabilize co-evolution, we introduce Evolutionary GRPO (E-GRPO), leveraging historical anchoring and revision safeguarding to mitigate advantage inflation. Additionally, we contribute CineTraj, a 40K cinematic trajectory dataset "},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29933","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29933.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29933","title":"Graph Topology Meets Energy-Driven Pathfinding: Preserving Contextual Consistency in Retrieval-Augmented Generation","authors":["Yufeng Li","Zaijing Li","Fuchen Zhang","Zongzong Wu","Ming Zhao","Fengxiao Tang"],"topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29933","tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) frequently integrates knowledge graphs (KGs) for complex multi-hop question answering. However, their performance is increasingly bottlenecked by the knowledge structuring and reasoning processes. (i) Severe semantic loss: Distilling continuous texts into discrete symbols strips away contextual nuances and narrative coherence. (ii) Pronounced modality mismatch: Fragmented graph structures clash with the continuous texts LLMs are pre-trained on, while rewriting them into natural language introduces generative noise and omits information. To address these limitations, we propose ThreadRAG, a semantic-topology-based RAG framework for context-preserving knowledge structuring and modality-native evidence reasoning. ThreadRAG introduces a Semantic Topology Mapper (STM), which organizes retrieved documents into context-intact Semantic Nodes and constructs a "},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29922","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29922.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29922","title":"Unintended Side Effects of Value Training in LLMs","authors":["Siddhesh Pawar","Chen Cecilia Liu","Anna Korhonen","Isabelle Augenstein"],"topics":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29922","tags":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"As LLMs are increasingly deployed in value-sensitive contexts, aligning them with human values has emerged as a post-training objective in NLP, aimed at instilling additional values and guiding model behaviour. However, current evaluations remain narrow. Because values are interconnected, it is unclear how fine-tuning for specific values alters unrelated capabilities and behaviours. In this work, we adapt four instruction-tuned LLMs across individual Hofstede's value dimensions and evaluate them on both cross-value transfer and broader behaviours. We find that value-specific training produces unintended side effects on untrained values, risk preferences, social cognition, and safety traits. Consequently, we suggest that value alignment after post-training should incorporate broader behavioral evaluations, and we highlight the need to investigate where additional value training might best"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.30042","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30042.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30042","title":"HGM-Dual: Lineage-Aware Guided Agent Harness Optimization for Long-Horizon Planning","authors":["Sudipta Paul","Pengrui Quan","Aounon Kumar","Nicholas Tzou","Vivek Kulkarni","Srinivas Chappidi"],"topics":["Planning","Evaluation","Alignment","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30042","tags":["Planning","Evaluation","Alignment","Reflection"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Harness optimization, which improves an agent's prompts, tools, and workflows, is an increasingly effective way to enhance agents built on frozen or closed-source LLMs. Leading approaches such as the Darwin--Godel Machine and the Huxley--Godel Machine (HGM) rely on recursive self-improvement and were developed mainly for coding, where the competence needed to improve the agent coincides with the competence being improved. Planning tasks, in which an agent must produce a sequence of tool calls that jointly satisfies interacting constraints, break this alignment. There, open-ended self-modification repeatedly explores ineffective changes and consumes substantial evaluation budgets. We introduce HGM-Dual, a diagnostic-guided framework for budget-efficient harness evolution for planning agents. First, HGM-Dual represents agent evolution as lineage-aware context. This context records what eac"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28770","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28770.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28770","title":"ReAPO: Retry-Aware Policy Optimization for Efficient Exploration in LLM Reasoning","authors":["Nghi Dao","Calder Morton-Ferguson","Noah Golowich","Adam Block"],"topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28770","tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"RLVR improves language-model reasoning, but commonly relies on independent stochastic rollouts for exploration, which can repeatedly sample similar failed strategies. Prior work has seen success with approaches that guide exploration by introducing dependence into the sampling process by including incorrect solutions in the context for subsequent attempts. Still, these methods introduce the additional question of how to assign credit to each attempt. We propose to view the credit assignment through the lens of Stochastic Shortest Paths, a framework in classical reinforcement learning that seeks to minimize the expected cumulative cost to reach a terminal state (in our case, a verified successful response). We show that, under suitable simplifying assumptions, this perspective naturally leads to a simple modification to GRPO, which we call Retry-Aware Policy Optimization (ReAPO). ReAPO al"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28779","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28779.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28779","title":"Second Thoughts: Exploring and Planning through Comparative Imagination in World Models","authors":["Hieu Tran","Hong Yu"],"topics":["LLM Training","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28779","tags":["LLM Training","Planning","Evaluation","World Model"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"World models let agents compare imagined outcomes before acting, but an agent must still decide which alternatives to explore and when a proposed plan should replace its learned policy. We introduce SDI (Sibling Disagreement in Imagination), which connects these decisions through a shared actor-based reference. SDI compares action sequences imagined from the same model state, using repeated rollouts of the actor's own sequence to measure variation under fixed behavior. This reference scales both the spread across candidate returns, which provides an exploration signal, and a proposed plan's predicted advantage, which governs action acceptance. The agent executes a planned action when its advantage exceeds the reference variability by a fixed factor; otherwise, it follows the actor. SDI reuses the base agent's world model, actor, and critic without requiring an additional disagreement ens"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29093","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29093.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29093","title":"Agentic Music Post-Production","authors":["Jaywon Koo","Junghyun Koo","Marco A. Martinez-Ramirez","Wei-Hsiang Liao","Seungheon Doh","Qiyu Wu","Vicente Ordonez","Yuki Mitsufuji"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29093","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Planning","Fine-tuning"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"We introduce MixAgent, an agentic pipeline for music post-production that follows the workflow of audio engineers to build interpretable and editable mixing plans guided by a reference song. A grouping agent first routes related input tracks, such as drum recordings, into subgroups. A planning agent then uses retrieved examples to choose and order audio effects, such as volume control and reverb, for tracks, subgroups, and the full mix. To refine the mix, a detector identifies what needs correction, and a critic proposes numerical parameter adjustments from tracks to the full mix. In our experiments, prompting recent multimodal models to listen and revise often worsens the mix, making reliable correction a central challenge. We therefore build MixCritic, a dataset of 45,657 repair critiques derived from parameters fitted to professional mixes, and train the critic with supervised fine-tu"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28883","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28883.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28883","title":"RL-IF: Tracking Non-local Training Data Influence for Online Reinforcement Learning","authors":["Shixuan Liu","Cheng Tang","Yuzheng Hu","Fan Wu","Han Zhao","Jiaqi W. Ma"],"topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Data Engine"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28883","tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Data Engine"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Online reinforcement learning (RL) has achieved significant success in decision-making tasks, but modern online RL remains highly sensitive to the quality of training experience. Understanding the contribution of individual training instances is therefore central to data-centric RL, with applications ranging from experience selection to policy interpretation. Existing data attribution methods for online RL primarily focus on local influence within a single training round, overlooking the non-local data influence across multiple training rounds. In this work, we formalize data attribution in online RL through a trajectory-level leave-one-out quantity, RL-LOO, which measures how one training sample influences a downstream target after subsequent online updates have taken place. We then derive RL-IF, a first-order estimator that propagates data influence through both optimization effects an"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29792","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29792.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29792","title":"MAPS: Macro-Adaptive Parallel Reasoning for Regulating Search Momentum in Retrieval-Augmented Generation","authors":["Xinze Li","Dingwen Zhang","Zhenghao Liu","Yukun Yan","Shuo Wang","Yu Gu","Ge Yu","Maosong Sun"],"topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29792","tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) improves the performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) by retrieving external knowledge. Recently, some RAG methods interleave reasoning and retrieval, enabling LLMs to progressively generate sub-queries and retrieve external knowledge. However, these methods may suffer from redundant or ineffective sub-queries and insufficiently diverse query exploration. Moreover, due to the tightly interleaved nature of reasoning and retrieval, it remains unclear how the evolving reasoning process influences query intent. To characterize the evolution of query intent during reasoning, we introduce search momentum, which captures the accumulated retrieval tendency induced by historical changes in generated sub-queries along the reasoning trajectory. Through momentum-based analysis, we find that the query generation process tends to continuously evolve along existi"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29244","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29244.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29244","title":"Failure Modes of Vision-Language Models for Human Activity Understanding in Thermal HRI","authors":["Han Hu","He Chen"],"topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Hallucination"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29244","tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Hallucination"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Thermal cameras enable robots to perceive human activities in darkness while revealing less personally identifiable appearance information and fewer details of the surrounding environment than RGB cameras. As vision-language models (VLMs), originally developed for RGB imagery, are increasingly integrated into embodied systems for scene understanding and high-level planning, thermal sensing offers a promising way to extend activity perception to low-light and privacy-sensitive settings. However, the failure modes of VLMs in thermal human activity understanding for human-robot interaction (HRI) remain underexplored. We fill this gap by evaluating seven locally deployable VLMs, including models developed for physical AI and robotics, on two person-centric thermal datasets. Our diagnostic tasks examine fine-grained action recognition, activity timeline reconstruction, and the ability to with"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29881","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29881.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29881","title":"Expandable Modality Experts for Medical Vision-Language Learning","authors":["DM BAPPY","Eunsu Kim","Hanjoo Lee","Sung Hak Lee","Sangjeong Ahn"],"topics":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29881","tags":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Evaluation","Alignment"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Learning across medical imaging modalities requires making use of images without paired text and accommodating new domains while retaining existing representations. We propose a medical vision-language technique built around an expandable bank of modality experts. Its Multiway Transformer combines shared visual computation with deterministically routed feed-forward experts and a separate text pathway. A modality-aware visual tokenizer supplies targets for masked pretraining on 19.4 million unpaired image rows across seven modalities; contrastive alignment then uses 3.23 million image-text pairs. Additional fundus and optical coherence tomography experts learn from paired data while the original image and text embedding paths remain frozen. With a ViT-Large backbone, the proposed technique achieves the best or tied-best result in 24 of 25 benchmark settings across nine modalities: 10 of 1"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.29232","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.29232.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29232","title":"Think-Capture: Active 3D Geometry Acquisition for Spatial Reasoning in Vision-Language Models","authors":["Junjie Wang","Zhihao Yuan","Yunhan Tang","Sheng Xu","Jiaqi Mao","Xiaoyu Ye","Yueru Luo","Xu Yan","Chun-Mei Feng","Zhen Li"],"topics":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Hallucination"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29232","tags":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Hallucination"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) excel at 2D recognition but often fail to maintain consistent 3D relations across sparse viewpoints, leading to spatial hallucinations under occlusion and perspective changes. Existing methods commonly fuse all frames with dense geometry, incurring substantial computation and context overhead while encouraging blind reliance on imperfect estimators. We present Think-Capture, a query-conditioned framework that first identifies task-critical objects and then acquires their geometry on demand. A 3D toolkit lifts zero-shot masks into a common coordinate frame, and a Symbolic 3D State Abstraction compresses object and camera states into structured text. Hierarchical Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) trains the VLM on cases in which geometry either helps or misleads, teaching it when to use tool evidence and when to fall back on visual cues. Think-Capture-"},{"id":"arxiv:2610.28776","submittedDate":"2026-10-20","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.28776.pdf","date":"2026-10-22","upvotes":0,"arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28776","title":"Complexity-Aware Surgical Video Reasoning Segmentation with Digital Twin and Context Memory","authors":["Yiqing Shen","Mathias Unberath"],"topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28776","tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"announceDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","abstract":"Conventional surgical segmentation relies on closed-set categories or explicit text queries, which cannot resolve implicit queries that require reasoning about workflows, instrument functions, and temporal relationships. To address these limitations, we propose SurgReasonSeg, a complexity-aware method that brings reasoning segmentation over digital twin (DT) representations to surgical video. Specifically, SurgReasonSeg constructs DT representations from surgical foundation models and trains two low-rank adapters (LoRA) of one large language model with reinforcement learning (RL), each specialized for a different query complexity. A lightweight router, also trained with RL, selects between the two adapters to balance latency against accuracy. SurgReasonSeg also sets the temporal context with an adaptive sliding window conditioned on query complexity and workflow phase, and retrieves rele"}],"archive":[],"latestPath":"data/latest.json"};
